{
  "version": "v1",
  "description": "Handshake. auth: the acceptor's decoding of the dialer's first frame. `auth` must be a non-empty string, else `invalid_auth` (close 1008, no ack). Capability parsing is lenient: a non-object `caps` is absent; `streams` is a non-negative JSON integer, else 0; `frames` keeps the known names `kit` and `cloudevents` in order, dropping others; a non-string `origin` is empty. `session` is strict, with the control-frame codes: not an object, a non-array `subscribe`, or a malformed `resume` (stream 1-128 bytes, `after` an offset token) is `bad_request`; a bad pattern `invalid_pattern`; a bad scope `invalid_scope`. Absent or null members normalize to null. ack: the dialer's decoding of the reply: `type` must be `auth_ok` or `session_error`, else the handshake fails; a `session_error` carries `code`. `mine` is the dialer's own streams version (required in every ack case). Only when the negotiated version min(`mine`, `caps.streams`) is at least 1 must `stream` hold `id` (1-128 bytes), offset tokens `oldest` and `head` with oldest <= head+1, and an integer `heartbeat_ms` in 1000..86400000, and a present `resume` be well formed, else the handshake fails (`ok` false, a protocol error, final for the dialer); below that, `stream` and `resume` are ignored and normalize to null. negotiate: link version is the minimum of both `streams` (no peer caps: 0); the send format is the configured one when there was no handshake or the peer listed it, else kit. ack_encode: the acceptor's reply; a dialer that sent no caps gets exactly {\"type\":\"auth_ok\"}. A failing ack case names its `error`: `rejected` (not an auth_ok or session_error reply) or the protocol error kind (`stream_info`, `oldest`, `heartbeat`, `resume`).",
